\caption{Standardized coefficients with clustered SE 95\% CIs for $\eta_{\max}$ (left) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (right) across 12 conditions. Filled circles: FDR $< 0.05$. $\eta_{\max}$ shows consistent positive effects (8/12 significant); $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ CIs predominantly contain zero (0/12 significant).}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\section{Robustness Checks: Subsampling and Specification}
\label{app:robustness_checks}

Table~\ref{tab:robustness_checks} summarizes regression results across model subsets and alternative specifications at $K{=}5$, testing whether the regime transition is robust to collinearity reduction and specification choice.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subset / Method} & $\boldsymbol{r}$ & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Trans.} \\
\midrule
Full bootstrap       & 0.81 & 0/12 & 7/12 & \checkmark \\
API-only (9) clust.  & 0.85 & 8/12 & 3/12 & --- \\
Cap-matched A (5A+4L) & 0.50 & ---  & 9/12 & \checkmark\checkmark \\
Cap-matched B (6A+3L) & 0.73 & ---  & 2/12 & --- \\
Fractional logit     & 0.81 & 5/12 & 9/12 & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Regime transition robustness at $K{=}5$ across model subsets and specifications. $r$: correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$. $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ columns show FDR-significant counts out of 12 conditions. Trans.: whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominates $\eta_{\max}$. API-only models ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$) show partially collinear predictors; capability-matched subsamples ($r{=}0.50$) strengthen the transition. Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} confirms OLS patterns with 93.1\% directional agreement.}
\label{tab:robustness_checks}
\end{table}

The API-only subset ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$) demonstrates that elevated collinearity between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ partially inflates standard errors: both predictors achieve moderate significance but neither dominates, and the transition does not replicate. Breaking collinearity through capability-matched subsampling (pool~A, $r{=}0.50$) strengthens $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s emergence to 9/12, exceeding the full-pool 7/12. Pool~B ($r{=}0.73$) falls near the decorrelation threshold, and the transition weakens accordingly. Random 9-model subsampling (1{,}000 trials) confirms an inverted-U: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance rate peaks when the subsample includes 3--4 local models (maximizing capability spread) and drops when the pool is too homogeneous (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\section{Random Subsample Analysis}
\label{app:random_subsample}

To test whether the regime transition depends on model pool composition, we randomly sample 9 of 15 models (1{,}000 trials), construct all $\binom{9}{5}$ panels, and run the bootstrap analysis at $K{=}5$. We stratify by the number of local models ($n_{\mathrm{local}} \in \{0, \ldots, 6\}$) in each subsample.

Under clustered standard errors, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is the dominant predictor in 51.4\% of subsamples (mean significance 1.85/12), with an inverted-U as a function of $n_{\mathrm{local}}$: subsamples with 3--4 local models (maximizing capability range) achieve the highest $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance rate, while all-API ($n_{\mathrm{local}}{=}0$) and heavily-local ($n_{\mathrm{local}}{\geq}5$) subsamples show reduced detection. Significance is attenuated relative to the full pool by smaller sample sizes (126 vs.\ 3{,}003 panels at $K{=}5$), but the transition direction holds in the majority of subsamples. This confirms that the transition's detectability depends on capability diversity in the model pool rather than pool size per se.

\section{Attacked $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ Stability}
\label{app:attacked_keff}

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is computed from clean (unattacked) pairwise MI (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), but used to predict vulnerability under attack. We verify this assumption by recomputing MI from judge outputs under each attack type and comparing clean vs.\ attacked $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ via Spearman rank correlation across all panels.

